2026-11-07T06:33:14.4685975Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4686617Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4687511Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4688180Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4688541Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4688719Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4689141Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4689405Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4689615Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4690000Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4690200Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4690317Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4690530Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4690887Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4691346Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4694283Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4694464Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4694773Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4695136Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4695897Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4696662Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4697311Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4697611Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4697922Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4698350Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4698840Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4708691Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4710264Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4717622Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4718430Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4718876Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4719570Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4720195Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4736305Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4737247Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4745721Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4746557Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4747182Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4747434Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4747640Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4748254Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4748519Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4749386Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4749774Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4749976Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4750405Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4750619Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4750868Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4751292Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4751468Z 
